@Jane Wheelaghan
Remove the OMs and HOMEROOMS becomes H(OM)ERO(OM)S, i.e., HEROS. A HERO is another name for the sandwich known variously through the US  as a gyro (from the Greek, pronounced HERO), hoagie, submarine AKA 'sub', po' boy,  etc. It is a baguette packed with sliced meats and veg.
ALAMODOME made me snort out loud when I got it. In the original it's the name of some immense sports containment site in the US, named for either the US rental car co. or the famed Texan battle. Removing the OM gets you ALAMODE which is a reasonable solution for 'trendy'.

@Darcey O’D
Yes, the American names are completely unknown, and 'baguette ' is used for both the actual bread stick and when it is filled. (A cheese and tomato baguette eg). I think more people tend to buy an ordinary sandwich which is always just 2 slices of bread with a filling. 

There's not such a huge variety of bread products in the uk, as in the us. Not such a variety of fillings either. A long time ago, when I was in NYC (1974-5) there were shops where you could order your own choice of bread and fillings (can you still do this?) but not over here. They are all pre-packed.

